            <ArticleTitle>Evolving treatment strategies for chronic refractory angina.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>259-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic refractory angina is a term used to describe patients who, despite optimal medical therapy, have both angina and objective evidence of ischaemia. It is estimated that 5-15% of the 12 million patients with chronic angina in the US meet the criteria for having refractory angina. This review focuses on the following evolving pharmacological therapies for chronic refractory angina: L-arginine, ivabradine, ranolazine, nicorandil and trimetazidine. Evolving devices and invasive procedures including enhanced external counterpulsation, spinal cord stimulation, and transmyocardial revascularisation are also briefly discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
